{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790533535,"nonce":"ec0/0gO+CQe01qrCKFqQUFP76ps=","title":"AOT module reload: limits left after the side-module reload","message":"What 580984a (c85602790b32311373531d561a08417dfa5aa627) leaves open. Field layout changes are tracked in 9ab7c22.\n\n- A subclass in another module keeps its copies of the methods it inherited from a reloaded class, so its instances run the old ones. The source reload (vm.reload_module) has the same limit.\n- Importers keep their copies of the reloaded module's non-class variables until they are themselves reloaded. Closures made before the reload keep their old code.\n- Each reload leaves the previous variable blob and constant table registered as GC roots, so what they hold stays alive for the rest of the run.\n- A reload object lowers the program's whole walk up to the changed module and relies on LLVM's dead-code elimination to drop the other modules, so reload compile time grows with the program.","files":null}]}